    },
    "body": "The original shared-core emitter collapsed ordered state components before its byte decisions. This change adds a versioned union/intersection of two calibrated angular caps, preserving the components until the decision. It retains finite integer/table serving and byte-exact legacy schema dispatch; recurrence and contextual access remain unchanged.\n\nThe one predeclared three-pass calibration **failed admission**: root errors 12/672 pass, but ASCII errors 220/660 miss the required fewer than 216. Construction accuracy improves from legacy 25/672 to 65/672; this is not language qualification. No joint fit, fresh holdout evaluation, new generation or promotion ran. Preserve legacy 5979f2e2, upgrade 331a9ef1 and calibrated 968cf841; retained 15baec48 stays authoritative.\n\nA subsequent read-only exhaustive hard-error audit finds minima of 10/672 and 202/660 on the same frozen states. The 202-error witness has worse branch NLL than calibration, so this establishes available decision capacity but does not establish a missed lower-loss optimum. No witness was installed. The next development action is to compare gate-compatible and unconstrained logistic-loss optima before selecting an optimizer change.\n\nValidation: release example build; 14 shared-core tests; a separate bitset/padding arithmetic test; actual saved-artifact capacity audit; legacy and schema-2 512-step zero-allocation censuses; formatting, claim wording and diff checks. The artifact round-trip and all original metrics reproduce. Sealed attempts, source/compiler/binary identities, negative artifact lineage and actual result are bound in docs/evidence/native_geometric_calibrated_emission_973.json.\n\nResources: 287,467/600,000 model/build/test ms, 26,107,904 added storage bytes within 256 MiB, no allowance extension or paid compute. The original dirty repair worktree, artifacts and evidence paths remain preserved. This is experimental source and negative evidence delivery, not serving-model promotion. Queue acknowledgements are not tests.\n\nReferences #973 and #964.\n",
